Fix D8: two saves in flight lose the CSS history entirely

KavachaUserCSS.listHistory() returned an empty array after two setCSS()
calls, so revertTo() had nothing to revert to and the history arm of patch
0025 could not be exercised at all. Live-apply and safe mode both passed,
which is why this was recorded as isolated to persistence.

Root cause is a time-of-check/time-of-use race in setCSS(), not the write:

  const previous = await this._loadCSS();   // cache, not disk
  if (previous.trim()) { await this._pushHistory(previous); }

_loadCSS() returns this._css when it is populated and reads the file when it
is null -- and this._css is set to null ASYNCHRONOUSLY, by the revision
observer that setCSS itself rings on the way out. So two setCSS() calls that
overlap both find the cache empty, both read the file as it was BEFORE either
of them wrote, both compute previous === "", and neither pushes anything. The
last writer wins the CSS file and the history stays empty. Nothing throws,
which is why it presented as a missing feature rather than a failure.

Overlap is not exotic. Marionette's execute_script does not await promises, so
the 0025 probe made exactly this call -- but so does the Studio's Apply button
racing the "Toggle Custom CSS Safe Mode" palette command, or any second save
issued before the first settles.

Reproduced against the real module with Services/IOUtils/PathUtils stubbed
(pref observers synchronous, IO asynchronous, as in Gecko):

  sequential (awaited)   1 history entry   <- the only passing shape
  concurrent             0 history entries
  fire-and-forget        0 history entries

The fix is a mutation queue. setCSS() and revertTo() now run through
_enqueue(), so only one mutation is ever in flight, and the "what am I
overwriting" read comes from _readCSSFromDisk() rather than the cache -- the
cache's whole problem is that its lifetime is controlled by the observer, not
by the caller. revertTo() takes the queue for its read AND its write together,
so a save landing in between cannot make it restore a different entry than the
one the user clicked.

Two related corrections, both of which were part of why the arm looked broken:

  * The empty baseline is now snapshotted. `if (previous.trim())` meant a user
    whose FIRST save was the one that broke their chrome had an empty history
    and nothing to revert to -- the exact situation the feature exists for.
    A true no-op (previous === css) is still skipped, so the history does not
    fill with duplicates.
  * _readHistory() swallowed every error, so an unreadable or corrupt history
    file was indistinguishable from an empty one. Missing-file is still silent
    (it is the first-run case); anything else now says so.

studio.js labels the baseline entry "(no custom CSS)", because a Restore button
with a blank preview beside it reads as a rendering bug.

After the fix, all four shapes record history, and the arm 0025 could never
reach now runs: save v1, save v2, revert to the oldest entry, land back on
empty CSS.

  sequential   2 entries   concurrent   2 entries
  fire-and-forget 2 entries   revert -> "" restored

Still to prove on a real build: the same sequence through about:studio's
Advanced tab against a live profile. The logic is verified; the profile path
is not.
